WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 99:59:59.999
Phase 4, Classical machine learning. The classical model zoo is still where most tabular problems are won. Start with the simplest model that could work, learn why it fails, and let complexity earn its keep with evidence. This phase has 8 lessons. Lesson 1: Frame the problem. ML is useful only when it improves a real decision. Define the prediction moment, available information, target, and cost of mistakes before writing model code. The question for this lesson: What decision should this model improve?. Most failed ML projects were doomed before anyone opened a notebook. The model was fine; the question was fuzzy. If nobody can say what decision changes when the prediction arrives, the project has no finish line. A model is a machine that turns information you have at one moment into a guess about something you do not know yet. Predicting tomorrow's umbrella sales is a different problem from predicting today's, because tomorrow's weather is not available today. Write the moment down and the features write themselves. You will learn: A feature is legal only if it exists before the prediction moment., A baseline is a required reference point, not a beginner-only exercise., The primary metric should encode the cost of being wrong.. Practice: "Write a one-page problem contract: user. Watch out for: Starting from an algorithm instead of a decision., Using information that only appears after the prediction moment., Changing the metric after seeing results.. You are ready when: Name the user., Define the target., Choose a primary metric.. Recommended video: A Gentle Introduction to Machine Learning. Lesson 2: Build a baseline. A baseline reveals whether there is signal and makes model complexity earn its cost. The question for this lesson: What is the simplest model that can win?. Before you build a Ferrari, check whether a bicycle already gets there. A baseline is the bicycle: predict the mean, predict the majority class, or use last week's value. It is embarrassingly simple and it keeps you honest. If a fancy model cannot beat the baseline, the problem is usually not the model. It is the labels, the split, the features, or the fact that there is simply no signal to find. That is a useful answer too. You will learn: Dummy estimators measure naive performance., Simple models are quick to debug., Complexity needs validation gain and operational justification.. Practice: "Compare a dummy estimator. Watch out for: Skipping the dummy model., Adding complexity before checking the labels., Comparing models trained on different splits.. You are ready when: Fit a dummy model., Set a threshold., Log the split.. Recommended video: Bias and Variance. Lesson 3: Linear regression. Linear regression is fast, interpretable, and often a very strong numeric-prediction baseline. Residuals reveal where its assumptions miss reality. The question for this lesson: When is a weighted relationship enough?. Linear regression draws the straight line that makes the total squared error as small as possible. It is the plain white shirt of ML: unglamorous, reusable, and right more often than people admit. Each coefficient is a conditional association: change one feature by one unit while the others stay fixed, and the prediction moves by roughly that amount. That is useful, but it is not a causal guarantee. The world rarely holds everything else fixed. You will learn: Coefficients are conditional associations, not causal guarantees., Residual plots reveal nonlinearity and unusual examples., Ridge adds L2 regularization for stability.. Practice: Compare linear and ridge regression. Plot residuals and explain the effect of alpha on coefficients.. Watch out for: Treating coefficients as causal proof., Ignoring residuals., Regularizing unscaled features.. You are ready when: Inspect residuals., Compare ridge., Check subgroups.. Recommended video: "Linear Regression. Lesson 4: Logistic regression and thresholds. Classification produces a score; a threshold turns it into a decision. The right threshold depends on error costs, capacity, prevalence, and calibration. The question for this lesson: What should a predicted probability trigger?. Linear regression will happily predict minus four hundred or plus one million. Logistic regression puts the output through a sigmoid, squashing it into the zero-to-one range so it can be read as a probability. The model gives you a score. You give the score a job. Moving the threshold changes the trade-off between false alarms and missed positives, and 0.5 is not a law of nature. It is a default that somebody forgot to question. You will learn: Precision and recall answer different questions., ROC-AUC ranks; PR-AUC is often stronger for rare positives., Calibration checks whether probabilities mean what they say.. Practice: "Plot PR and calibration curves. Watch out for: Using accuracy for rare events., Assuming 0.5 is a special threshold., Confusing ranking quality with calibration.. You are ready when: Compare thresholds., Choose a metric from cost., Check calibration.. Recommended video: Logistic Regression. Lesson 5: Decision trees. Trees learn nonlinear rules and interactions, but deep trees can memorize training data quickly. The question for this lesson: When should a model split instead of draw a line?. A decision tree is a flowchart learned from data. At each node it asks the yes/no question that makes the two groups as pure as possible. Keep asking until the groups are useful or the tree runs out of manners. Trees are wonderful because they can capture thresholds and interactions without you engineering them by hand. They are dangerous for the same reason: give one enough depth and it will memorize the training set, including the noise. You will learn: Splits reduce impurity or error., Depth and pruning control memorization., Feature importance is not causal importance.. Practice: "Train shallow. Watch out for: Unbounded depth., Reading importance causally., Ignoring unstable splits.. You are ready when: Tune depth with CV., Compare against baseline., Inspect paths.. Recommended video: Decision Trees. Lesson 6: Random forests and boosting. Random forests average diverse trees; boosting corrects prior errors sequentially. Both can excel on tabular data but still need validation, latency budgets, and restraint. The question for this lesson: When do many models beat one?. One tree is opinionated. A forest is a committee: many trees, each trained on a slightly different view of the data, then averaged. The committee is usually calmer and more accurate than any single member. Boosting works differently. It builds trees in sequence, and each new tree focuses on the mistakes the previous ones made. Random forests reduce variance; boosting reduces bias. Both can be brilliant, and both can be tuned into a small overfitting factory. You will learn: Bagging reduces variance., Boosting improves residual errors., Depth, learning rate, and early stopping control complexity.. Practice: Compare random forest and gradient boosting on fixed folds. Tune only two parameters with a written hypothesis for each.. Watch out for: Blind hyperparameter sweeps., Different folds for each model., Ignoring inference time.. You are ready when: Use CV., Use early stopping., Measure latency.. Recommended video: "Random Forests. Lesson 7: KNN, SVMs, and the meaning of similarity. KNN and SVMs depend on geometry. They can be excellent when scaling, distance, margin, and representation match the task. The question for this lesson: What does similarity mean in your data?. K-nearest neighbors asks a very human question: who are you standing next to? To predict a new point, it finds the closest training examples and lets them vote. No equation is learned; the dataset is the model. A support vector machine asks a different question: where is the widest street between the classes? It chooses the boundary with the largest margin, which tends to generalize well. Kernels let that street bend without leaving the math behind. You will learn: KNN predicts from nearby examples., SVMs maximize a margin., High dimensions make raw distance less informative.. Practice: Run KNN with and without scaling. Then vary SVM C and gamma and record the boundary and validation score.. Watch out for: Unscaled distance features., Ignoring KNN inference cost., Treating kernel settings as magic.. You are ready when: Scale features., Test sensitivity., Measure prediction cost.. Recommended video: "K-nearest neighbors. Lesson 8: Clustering and structure without labels. K-means, hierarchical clustering, and PCA find structure without labels. The hard part is deciding whether the structure is useful or just an artifact of scale. The question for this lesson: What groups exist when nobody gave you the answers?. Clustering is unsupervised, which means there is no ground truth to appeal to. The algorithm will always return clusters, even on random noise. Your job is to decide whether those groups mean anything to the problem. Scale the features, choose a distance, and inspect the result. A cluster is a hypothesis, not a label. Validate it with domain knowledge, stability across seeds, and a downstream decision it actually changes. You will learn: K-means assumes roughly spherical clusters and a chosen k., PCA compresses correlated features into directions of variance., Silhouette and stability help, but domain usefulness decides.. Practice: "Cluster a customer or product table. Watch out for: Clustering unscaled features and letting one column dominate., Treating cluster IDs as true labels., Choosing k only by an elbow plot.. You are ready when: Scale features., Check stability., Explain the segments.. Recommended video: StatQuest: K-means clustering. You can frame a prediction problem, pick a sensible model, and defend the choice. Take your time with each lesson and practice the examples.
